This new paper treats LLM adoption like a contagious process—think “cognitive virus.” Using epidemic math, the authors model how routine AI use can tip entire populations from autonomy to deep dependence in a snap, with average competence collapsing abruptly at a critical threshold (λ_TC = ρ+κ). Even a small rise in social adoption pressure can trigger a rapid, hard-to-reverse shift to widespread offloading of thinking.

The scary part? Once society crosses the tipping point, rolling back dependence is much harder—a classic hysteresis/lock-in effect. But the same model shows how “cognitive immunization”—like enforced periods of unaided work, friction in UI, or curricula that reward independent reasoning—can raise the bar for runaway dependence, and help preserve human skill.

A must-read if you care about AI, education, or how to keep humans in the loop as LLMs go viral.

